# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
# `node_modules/` WITHOUT the leading slash, so nested workspaces are covered too.
# The root-anchored `/node_modules` left every `packages/*/node_modules` exposed,
# and that is how 17 MB of cross-platform Prisma engines
# (`linux-musl-openssl-3.0.x/libquery-engine`) sat untracked-but-visible, one
# `git add -A` away from a commit. These are exactly the foreign-native binaries
# the release-payload gates exist to keep out of artifacts -- they must not be in
# the repository either.
node_modules/
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# package-manager caches (npm writes these into the package that triggered them)
.npm/
.eslintcache

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
**/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.local.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
dist/
**/dist/
.turbo/

# prisma generated client (regenerated by `npm run build:packages`)
**/generated/

# sqlite databases + journals
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal

# runtime tunnel data (binaries, logs, keys, configs)
tunnels/*
!tunnels/examples/

# local desktop tool state (not part of the project)
.freebuff/
.remember/

# local database directory. The *.db files are already covered by the rule above,
# but a test that writes its stdout beside them (`.data/test-out.txt`) is NOT --
# `**/*.db` matches by extension, and nothing covered the directory itself. So a
# blanket `git add -A` would have committed a 15 KB run of test output.
#
# These databases hold scrypt passwordHash values and live Session rows. Nothing in
# `.data/` is source: it is machine-local runtime state, regenerated by any test
# run, and it must never enter a commit.
.data/

# local agent loop state (machine-local, see .opencode/ralph-loop.local.md)
.opencode/

# browser-gate run output: per-suite logs and verdict.json. Uploaded as a CI
# artifact, never committed -- a failing browser suite can print a page dump, and
# even scrubbed that is not source.
.agent/tmp-smoke/

# Ad-hoc probe harnesses written into .agent/ during a session. Same reasoning as
# tmp-smoke: these are scratch, they accumulate silently because .agent/ is
# otherwise all tracked evidence, and a throwaway shell script that reaches the
# host over the network is exactly the file that must not be committed. Three
# such scripts (tmp-vps-auth.sh, tmp-vps-rollback.sh, tmp-upload/) sat
# untracked-but-unignored and would have shipped in a release commit.
#
# No negation for tmp-smoke: `.agent/tmp-*` re-ignores the directory, and a
# `!.agent/tmp-smoke/` line silently re-includes everything under it. Adding one
# un-ignored 134 browser-gate logs in the very commit meant to close the gap --
# git check-ignore on a directory reports the pattern, not the files beneath it,
# so this only shows up by asking git for the individual paths.
.agent/tmp-*
